  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nUpper low over western Quebec is wobbling east out of the region\nand releasing it's influence. Narrow surface ridge will wash\nacross the Lower Peninsula tonight and early Tuesday. As a\nconsequence the fresh northwest flow will quell out early this\nevening. The light flow and very good radiative cooling potential\nwill support some fog development tonight. Fog will lift into a\ncumulus field by late morning. Otherwise, a quiet late summer day\nis in store Tuesday with partly cloudy skies and highs around 80F.\n\nThe next shortwave in the westerlies, currently over the southern\nCanadian Prairies will carve into the Great Lakes region on\nWednesday. The wave will undergo some level of capturing by the\nlarger Hudson Bay closed low over the next day or so. This process\nwill induce height falls to the north of the wave centroid,\ncoaxing the best forcing north. Therefore, the main forcing \nacross Western Lower Michigan will be the attendant cold front - \nwith the primary wave ascent shunted north across the northern \nLakes region.\n\nAn uptick in south-southwest flow on Wednesday in advance of the\ncold front, will squeeze in some better moisture content and\nactionable instability - MLCAPE values approaching 1-kJ/kg. \nShowers and scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop along \nthe advancing buoyancy gradient during the mid/late afternoon, \nwith a second impulse of development along the cold front during \nthe evening hours. Still plenty of timing wiggle room in the \nforecast, given this system still has two more diurnal convective\ncycles to augment the forcing before arrival. Severe potential\nwill be limited with only modest deep layer shear due to weakening\nmid-level flow.\n\nNot much a of cool down on Thursday behind the front with ample \nlate summer sun. A stray shower or storm is certainly possible\nalong and south of I-96, with some lingering modest instability\nlingering.\n\nOverall flow pattern appears poised to support greater ridging\naloft over the Great Lakes region as we progress through the\nupcoming weekend and into next week. Global ensembles are starting\nto gel around the notion of a period of above average temps\nstarting Sunday.",
